<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Balkans</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Balkan</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Balkans are the peninsula of south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> between the Adriatic, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a> and Black seas. The region was a core part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a> and later of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>, which ruled it for centuries. Nationalist movements and great-power rivalry made the Balkans the scene of Ottoman decline and of the crisis that drew the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austro-Hungarian Empire</a> into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/balkans.jpg" alt="Balkans" width="320" height="267" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ikonact · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Geographic_map_of_Balkan_Peninsula.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Balkans take their name from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkan-mountains">Balkan Mountains</a>, which stretch across the whole of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>. The Balkan Peninsula is bordered by the Adriatic Sea in the northwest, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ionian-sea">Ionian Sea</a> in the southwest, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean Sea</a> in the south, the Turkish Straits in the east and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> in the northeast, and has an area of about 470,000 square kilometres.</p><p>Its northern border is variously defined, often as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sava">Sava</a> and Kupa rivers. Most of the area is covered by mountain ranges running from the northwest to the southeast, among them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkan-mountains">Balkan Mountains</a>, the Rila-Rhodope massif, the Dinaric Alps and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pindus">Pindus</a> range. The highest point is Musala, at 2,925 metres, in the Rila range in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>.</p><p>The concept of the Balkan Peninsula was created by the German geographer August Zeune in 1808, who mistakenly considered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkan-mountains">Balkan Mountains</a> the dominant mountain system of south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. Geographers such as Carl Ritter objected, and in the 19th century the term was a synonym for Rumelia, the European provinces of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>. Its definition is geopolitical rather than geographical, and the term has acquired a stigmatised, pejorative meaning related to Balkanization; the region may instead be called Southeast Europe.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Balkans have been inhabited since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paleolithic">Paleolithic</a> and were the route by which farming spread from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-east">Middle East</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a>, in the 7th millennium BCE. The first known Neolithic culture of Old Europe, the Kakanj culture, appeared in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia">Bosnia</a> and covers the period from 6795 to 4900 BCE. The region of the Old Europe or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> civilisation peaked between 5000 and 3500 BCE.</p><p>In the Early Middle Ages the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a> was the dominant state in the region, militarily and culturally. In the second half of the 9th century the Byzantine missionaries <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyril-and-methodius">Cyril and Methodius</a> spread the Byzantine variety of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> to the majority of the previously pagan inhabitants; it was adopted first by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgarians">Bulgarians</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a>, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romanians">Romanians</a> joining a little later. In 1912 and 1913 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> and Montenegro allied against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-balkan-war">First Balkan War</a> and partitioned almost all its remaining European territories, and events led to the creation of an independent Albanian state.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> began the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-balkan-war">Second Balkan War</a> by attacking its former allies, and collapsed when the Greek army invaded and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a> intervened; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> used the chance to recapture <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-thrace">Eastern Thrace</a>, fixing its western borders that still stand today as part of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Balkans” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under Bayan I they besieged and took the Byzantine fortress of Sirmium in 582 after a long blockade, then captured Singidunum and raided deep into the <b>Balkans</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/avar-khaganate">Avar Khaganate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>It rested on the stability of the gold solidus (later called the nomisma), on Constantinople's position at the crossroads of intercontinental trade, and on the agricultural surpluses of Anatolia and the <b>Balkans</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Its name, which reaches us through Greek and Latin writers, has no connection with Albania in the <b>Balkans</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/caucasian-albania">Caucasian Albania · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>After reunification Germany sent peacekeepers to the <b>Balkans</b> and troops to Afghanistan with NATO.</blockquote><a href="/empire/federal-republic-of-germany">Federal Republic of Germany · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In 681 Byzantium made peace and agreed to pay an annual tribute, in effect recognising an independent state on former imperial territory south of the Danube, a concession without precedent in the <b>Balkans</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/first-bulgarian-empire">First Bulgarian Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The new republic governed a mountainous peninsula at the southern end of the <b>Balkans</b> with the longest coastline in the Mediterranean, up to 227 inhabited islands and some ten million people, its capital Athens and its co-capital Thessaloniki.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hellenic-republic">Hellenic Republic · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
